  "read": {
    "headline": "The ten-year had its worst day since April 2025, on a survey and an auction.",
    "standfirst": "Wednesday was the bond market's day. A flash composite PMI of 58.4 — the highest since July 2021, with input prices at their highest since October 2022 — took the ten-year yield up 14.6bp to 5.11%, its largest one-day rise since April 2025 and highest close since July 2007; the five-year closed above 5% for the first time since 2007 and a $70 billion five-year auction stopped at 5.033%, the highest since 2006. Every sector but energy fell: the S&P 500 lost 0.75% to 7,706.03, the Nasdaq 1.13% off its record, the Russell 2000 1.77%. Brent rose 3.86% back above $100 after Iran said Hormuz stays shut, and the dollar closed at an eight-week high.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "Every bond row is red, and the long end is reddest.",
        "text": "The 20-year-plus Treasury ETF fell 1.58% on Wednesday, the 7–10 year 1.06%, investment-grade credit 1.14% and the aggregate 0.87%. The ten-year yield rose to 5.11%, its largest one-day rise since April 2025 and its highest close since July 2007; the five-year closed above 5% for the first time since 2007 and the two-year at 4.85% is the highest since May 2024. Over a month the two-year is up 16.31% and the ten-year 10.13%. TLT is now down 9.58% over twelve months, the worst row in the group."
      },
      {
        "title": "A survey did this — and then an auction confirmed it.",
        "text": "The flash composite PMI printed 58.4, the highest since July 2021, with input prices at their highest since October 2022. Two hours later the $70 billion five-year auction stopped at 5.033%, the highest since June 2006, about 3bp above where the issue traded at the deadline, with dealers left holding 15.8%. October hike odds went from 49% a week ago to 73%. Ten of eleven sectors fell; utilities lost 1.92%, real estate 1.55%, discretionary 1.50%, and the Russell 2000 fell 1.77%. Energy, at +0.95%, was the exception."
      },
      {
        "title": "Oil snapped six days of falls on a speech and a struck ship.",
        "text": "Brent rose 3.86% to settle at $103.08 and WTI 1.81% to $92.16 after Iran's president told the UN the country would not surrender and the strait would stay closed until its conditions were met, and a cargo vessel was hit inside Hormuz. The EIA reported a 3.0 million barrel commercial build against a draw expected, and crude rose anyway. Brent's five-day column now reads −0.64%; a week of de-escalation pricing has been given back in a session. Natural gas, up 9.89% over five days, never joined the sell-off in the first place."
      },
      {
        "title": "The dollar at an eight-week high took the emerging-market board with it.",
        "text": "South Africa fell 3.92%, Korea 3.62%, Australia 2.51%, Brazil 2.33% and Taiwan 2.27%; the EM ETF lost 2.01% and developed ex-US 1.60%, against 0.72% for the United States. Only Indonesia, Turkey and Qatar rose. The dollar index closed at 101.23 as US yields rose faster than anyone else's, and Korea — up 1.83% on Tuesday, 131.95% over twelve months — gave back two days in one. A rates shock in Treasuries is a currency shock everywhere else first."
      },
      {
        "title": "The only thing on the board that went up a lot was Litecoin.",
        "text": "Litecoin rose 9.87% on the day and is up 16.58% over five days and 35.98% over a month — the largest moves on the board in every window under three months. Bitcoin was flat at −0.23% while stocks and bonds both fell; the crypto group as a whole was green on a day almost nothing else was. It is still down 25.74% over twelve months. Correlation broke in crypto's favour on Wednesday, which is not the same as crypto being a hedge."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold did not hedge any of it.",
        "text": "On a day bonds fell 1% and stocks fell 1%, gold was unchanged at $4,319.70; it is down 2.38% over five days and 7.98% over a month, and silver 11.12% over six months. A real-yield shock — the ten-year up 10% in a month against a dollar at eight-week highs — is the one environment gold does not work in, and this year has been almost entirely that. Copper, down 0.38% on the day, is still up 40.44% over twelve months; the industrial metal has beaten the monetary one all year."
      }
    ]
  },
